body:
To avoid a late integration surprise, this is what vt/n2hwenc_vt.c will call through one adapter (vt/n2hwenc_client.c). It is the shape your TASK already names (connect HELLO, enc_open with in/out sizes, enc_frame(y, c, strides, ...), enc_set, close). If your final header differs, only my adapter changes; nothing else in my package calls the client.

struct n2vcd_conn;                 /* your connection object */
struct n2vcd_session;              /* one encoder instance */
struct n2vcd_enc_params {
    uint32_t codec;                /* N2VCD_CODEC_H264 / N2VCD_CODEC_HEVC */
    uint32_t width, height;
    uint32_t fps_num, fps_den, bitrate;
    uint32_t gop;                  /* 0 = default */
    uint32_t profile, level;       /* struct n2vcd_open fields: H.264 profile_idc/level_idc; HEVC profile_idc and level_idc=30*level */
    uint32_t flags;                /* bit 0: real time / low latency */
};
int n2vcd_connect(struct n2vcd_conn **out, const char *socket_path, const char *dev_path); /* NULL,NULL = phone relay; -errno */
int n2vcd_enc_open(struct n2vcd_conn *c, const struct n2vcd_enc_params *p, struct n2vcd_session **out,
                   uint32_t *in_buf_bytes, uint32_t *out_buf_bytes);
int n2vcd_enc_frame(struct n2vcd_session *s, const uint8_t *y, const uint8_t *cbcr,
                    uint32_t luma_stride, uint32_t chroma_stride, uint64_t pts, int force_key,
                    uint8_t *out, uint32_t out_capacity, uint32_t *out_bytes, int *key);
int n2vcd_enc_set(struct n2vcd_session *s, uint32_t bitrate, uint32_t fps_num, uint32_t fps_den, int force_key);
int n2vcd_close(struct n2vcd_session *s);
int n2vcd_disconnect(struct n2vcd_conn *c);

Semantics I rely on: (1) every call returns 0 or a negative errno, never a positive N2VCD_E_*; (2) enc_frame writes one Annex B access unit (start codes, the daemon may prepend parameter sets on key frames - header_bytes or not does not matter, I scan the NALs), out_bytes <= out_capacity and the call fails (-ENOSPC) rather than truncating; (3) the session is not thread-safe, but my object holds its own mutex so only one call per session happens at a time (two sessions may run in two threads - that is my TSan test); (4) enc_open's in/out sizes are the exact buffer sizes you allocate, so enc_frame's y/cbcr may be copied into your DMA input and out_capacity never exceeds yours; (5) a vanished server (E_GUEST / EPIPE) is session-fatal and every later call returns an error - per lead's decision; my object treats the first such error as fatal for the session and reports N2_kVTVideoEncoderMalfunctionErr, VideoToolbox then falls back to software for the next session.

Question: with the interface above, is enc_frame's y/cbcr expected to be host pointers that you copy into the shared DMA buffer, or do you want me to hand you DMA handles you allocate? Your TASK text says y/cbcr, so I assume the copy is yours. I do not need an answer to start; this is the contract my fake client implements today.
